    "q-110760": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110760",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each subset.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. For instance, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x-4y=1$ since $0-4\\cdot 0=0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=-2y$ since $0=-2\\cdot 0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. No point in ${R}^{3}$ satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0.$ Hence, $C=∅.$ The empty set is not a subspace since it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
